x86: load_unaligned_zeropad() for l4h without a faulting page crossing

x86's load_unaligned_zeropad() lets a word load cross into an unmapped
next page and returns zeroes for the missing bytes from the exception
table (EX_TYPE_ZEROPAD).  A fault in the l4h guest kernel goes to the
host and stops the guest instead, and the direct map has holes where RAM
blocks became seL4 objects, so on l4h a word that crosses a page is
loaded by arch/l4h/x86/lib/word-at-a-time.c, which checks the next page
first.  Changing this header rather than shadowing it from arch/l4h
makes Kbuild and ccache rebuild everything that inlines the helper.

--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/word-at-a-time.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/word-at-a-time.h
@@ -67,10 +67,25 @@
  * and the next page not being mapped, take the exception and
  * return zeroes in the non-existing part.
  */
+#ifdef CONFIG_L4H
+#include <asm/page.h>
+
+unsigned long l4h_load_unaligned_zeropad_cross(const void *addr);
+#endif
+
 static inline unsigned long load_unaligned_zeropad(const void *addr)
 {
 	unsigned long ret;
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_L4H
+	/*
+	 * A fault in the l4h guest kernel stops the guest instead of
+	 * reaching the exception table, so a word crossing into the next
+	 * page is loaded out of line (arch/l4h/x86/lib/word-at-a-time.c).
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(((unsigned long)addr & (PAGE_SIZE - 1)) > PAGE_SIZE - sizeof(long)))
+		return l4h_load_unaligned_zeropad_cross(addr);
+#endif
 	asm volatile(
 		"1:	mov %[mem], %[ret]\n"
 		"2:\n"
